WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
The first film marked both ends and joined blue exons.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.660
If those joins change, must the protein's reading frame change too?

3
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.680
Follow different copies of one gene's transcript as each is spliced its own way.

4
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.140
First, in a normal cell, regulatory signals can steer the spliceosome to different

5
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.140
junctions.

6
00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:27.343
Splicing need not make one pattern.

7
00:00:28.563 --> 00:00:33.923
A cell may skip an exon, shift a splice site, choose between exons, or retain an

8
00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:37.943
intron. Each choice makes a different messenger RNA.

9
00:00:39.823 --> 00:00:43.003
Regulatory signals can make these patterns differ among tissues.

10
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:47.347
A sequence change can weaken a normal splice site.

11
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.607
Nearby sites then compete differently.

12
00:00:51.947 --> 00:00:55.227
A weakened splice site most often causes exon skipping.

13
00:00:56.707 --> 00:01:00.407
Less often, a cryptic site takes over or the intron is retained.

14
00:01:01.807 --> 00:01:04.147
One allele can make several products at once.

15
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:10.847
One studied beta-thalassemia allele made two abnormal RNAs in cultured cells.

16
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:14.247
The main product kept part of an intron.

17
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.047
A smaller amount joined nonadjacent exons.

18
00:01:19.407 --> 00:01:23.587
One changed splice signal shifted competition in more than one way.

19
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.817
Length is only the first consequence.

20
00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:32.357
In coding sequence, ribosomes read bases in groups called codons.

21
00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:38.277
Changing the length can shift every downstream group, changing many amino acids.

22
00:01:39.597 --> 00:01:43.577
A shift after one junction can change every later codon boundary.

23
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.680
This outlined exon contains only complete codon groups.

24
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.740
Imagine that splicing skips it.

25
00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.880
Use the ruler, not the exon count.

26
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.900
Will the downstream groups stay aligned, or will every boundary move?

27
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:07.173
They stay aligned.

28
00:02:08.573 --> 00:02:13.553
Removing complete codon groups changes the message's length but not its downstream

29
00:02:13.553 --> 00:02:18.873
grouping. Whole triplets leave, so the next base begins a codon normally.

30
00:02:20.213 --> 00:02:24.413
The new junction can still matter, because sequence determines what it encodes.

31
00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:30.077
Now skip a coding segment with one extra base beyond complete groups.

32
00:02:31.297 --> 00:02:33.037
Every downstream boundary shifts.

33
00:02:34.277 --> 00:02:36.857
The reading marker now meets different base groups.

34
00:02:38.077 --> 00:02:42.637
A premature stop becomes likely, although only the sequence can show where one

35
00:02:42.637 --> 00:02:43.637
appears.

36
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.340
The remainder test applies to net changes inside coding sequence.

37
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.680
It does not predict effects of untranslated changes.

38
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.860
It also cannot tell whether an RNA is exported, degraded, or translated.

39
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.680
Those fates require evidence beyond a length calculation.

40
00:03:04.833 --> 00:03:09.933
Processing still marks transcript ends and chooses which segments join into

41
00:03:09.933 --> 00:03:14.733
messenger RNA. For coding consequences, keep a second rule.

42
00:03:16.173 --> 00:03:20.573
Coding-length changes keep the downstream frame only in multiples of three.

43
00:03:21.833 --> 00:03:24.793
It applies only when the changed nucleotides are coding.

44
00:03:26.493 --> 00:03:28.593
Sequence decides the actual protein outcome.

45
00:03:29.573 --> 00:03:31.293
That is the scope of this rule.
